  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14096050/assets/9374015d12e523424af9766b4f0abe920ca8109cd808f277e8341f182bc78747.png)\n\nAll of the edges of a cube are shrinking at a rate of $4\\text{\u00a0cm/s}.$ How fast is the surface area decreasing when each edge is $3\\text{\u00a0cm}?$",
  "worked_solution": "Let $x$ be the side of the cube, let $S$ be its surface area, and let $V$ be its volume. All of the variables change with time. In particular, we are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-4\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=3\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nWe want to find the rate of change of $S$ knowing the rate of change of $x.$ So, we start with the formula for the surface area of the cube:\n\n$$\nS=6{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(S) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =12x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =12x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =12(3)(-4) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =-144.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cube's surface area is decreasing at a rate of $144\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{s}.$",
